eitas", "sourceName": "Lançarote de Freitas", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "bay-of-arguin", "targetName": "Bay of Arguin, Mauritania", "context": "Lançarote de Freitas conducted his first major slave raid in 1444, launching a surprise attack on a fishing village in the Bay of Arguin."}, {"sourceSlug": "lancarote-de-freitas", "sourceName": "Lançarote de Freitas", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "european-colonialism-in-africa", "targetName": "European Colonialism in Africa", "context": "Lançarote de Freitas's profitable slaving ventures contributed to the economic motivations and methods that would characterize subsequent European colonialism in Africa."}], "places": [{"name": "Lagos, Portugal", "role": "Home base"}, {"name": "Bay of Arguin, Mauritania", "role": "Primary raid site"}, {"name": "Senegambian Coast, West Africa", "role": "Exploration & raiding area"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Lançarote de Freitas", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "lançarote-de-freitas"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3076777", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lan%C3%A7arote_de_Freitas"], "tags": ["Explorers & Navigators", "cultural-diffusion", "environmental-history", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Lançarote de Freitas was a pivotal figure in the early establishment of the direct European maritime slave trade, initiating systematic raids that brought hundreds of West Africans to Portugal. His expeditions laid the operational and economic groundwork for the transatlantic slave trade, profoundly shaping the trajectory of European colonial expansion and the suffering of millions.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-27T00:49:18Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30228249008", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Lançarote de Freitas, better known as Lançarote de Lagos or Lançarote da Ilha, was a 15th-century Portuguese explorer and slave raider from Lagos, Portugal. He was the leader of two large Portuguese slaving raids on the West African coast in 1444–1446.", "newValue": "Lançarote de Freitas, a prominent 15th-century Portuguese navigator and slave trader, emerged from Lagos, Portugal, to become a central figure in the nascent European transatlantic slave trade. Operating under the patronage of Prince Henry the Navigator, his expeditions between 1444 and 1446 marked a critical escalation in the systematic capture and transportation of West Africans to Portugal, profoundly shaping the economic and moral trajectory of the Age of Discovery. His actions transformed incidental raiding into an organized, profitable enterprise, setting a brutal precedent for future colonial exploitation.

In 1444, Lançarote commanded a fleet of six caravels, sailing from Lagos to the Bay of Arguin (modern-day Mauritania), where his forces launched a brutal surprise attack on a local fishing village, capturing 235 individuals. These captives were then brought back to Lagos, where they were publicly paraded and divided, with a fifth reserved for Prince Henry. A second, equally devastating expedition in 1445-1446, involving fourteen vessels, further solidified the profitability and tactical blueprint of these raids, extending Portuguese reach along the Senegambian coast and capturing hundreds more, despite encountering increased resistance from local populations.

Lançarote de Freitas's expeditions were instrumental in establishing the economic viability and operational model for the Portuguese slave trade, directly influencing the development of large-scale chattel slavery in the Atlantic world. His success provided crucial funding and impetus for subsequent Portuguese explorations and colonial ventures along the African coast, legitimizing the horrific practice of human trafficking as a cornerstone of imperial expansion. The wealth generated from these early slave raids fueled further maritime innovation and territorial claims, embedding the institution of slavery deep within the fabric of European colonial ambition."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-27T00:49:18Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30228249008", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Curiosity about unknown territories and navigation advances", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The Portuguese Crown's desire for new trade routes and resources spurred exploration along the West African coast.", "Prince Henry the Navigator's strategic vision and financial backing provided the impetus and infrastructure for maritime expeditions.", "The existing trans-Saharan slave trade demonstrated the economic potential of human trafficking, influencing Portuguese commercial interests."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-27T00:49:18Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30228249008", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Expanded geographic knowledge and cultural exchange", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "Classical"}], "newValue": ["Lançarote's expeditions dramatically increased the volume of enslaved Africans brought to Portugal, establishing a direct mari